Markdown Syntax
***************

.[perex]
Do you write Markdown the way you know it from GitHub or README files? Texy reads it according to the CommonMark specification, GitHub Flavored Markdown extensions included, and on top of that it typesets the text: it adds proper quotes, dashes and non-breaking spaces, and repairs broken HTML. Here you'll find everything you can write in Markdown.


Cheat Sheet
===========

| [#Text Formatting]                   | syntax
|-----------------------------------------------
| [Bold text |#Text Formatting]        | ''**bold text**'' or ''__bold text__''
| [Italic |#Text Formatting]           | ''*italic*'' or ''_italic_''
| [Inline code |#Text Formatting]      | ''`code`''
| [Strikethrough |#Text Formatting] 🔸 | ''~~struck out~~''
| [#Links]                             | ''[text](https://example.com)''
| [#Images]                            | ''![description](image.jpg)''
|-----------------------------------------------
| Blocks
|-----------------------------------------------
| [#Headings]                          | ''# Heading'' <br> ''## Smaller heading''
| [#Lists]                             | ''- item'' <br> ''1. item''
| [Task lists |#Lists] 🔸              | ''- [x] done'' <br> ''- [ ] to do''
| [#Blockquotes]                       | ''> quote''
| [Code blocks |#Code]                 | <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;php</code> <br> … <br> <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;</code>
| [#Tables] 🔸                         | ''\| cell \| cell \|''
| [#Horizontal Rule]                   | ''---''

Syntaxes marked 🔸 belong to the GitHub Flavored Markdown (GFM) extension. They work wherever the website turns them on, which a developer does with a single call, as described in [configuration |configuration#Markdown].


Paragraphs and Line Breaks
==========================

A paragraph is one or more lines that follow each other directly. Texy joins them into one continuous text, so you don't have to worry about a sentence breaking where you happened to press Enter in your editor. A new paragraph starts only after a blank line:

```
This is the first paragraph. It can easily span several lines
and Texy joins them into one.

Only here, after a blank line, does the second paragraph begin.
```

Need just a line break, say in an address? End the line with two spaces or a backslash:

```
Jane Smith··
12 Orchard Lane\
Springfield, IL 62704
```

The dots `··` stand for the two spaces, which would otherwise be invisible. Since you can't see them in your editor either, the backslash is the more reliable choice.


Text Formatting
===============

| syntax                               | result
|---------------------------------------------------------
| ''*italic*'' or ''_italic_''         | *italic*
| ''**bold**'' or ''__bold__''         | **bold**
| ''***bold italic***''                | ***bold italic***
| ''`code`''                           | `code`
| ''~~struck out~~'' 🔸                | <del>struck out</del>

The underscore only works at word boundaries, so `file_name_txt` stays as it is. The asterisk can emphasize part of a word too: `**wonder**ful`.


Headings
========

A heading starts with a hash sign. The more hashes, the smaller the heading, from `#` for the top level down to `######` for the sixth:

```
# How We Dried Plums

## The Harvest

## Drying
```

First- and second-level headings can also be written by underlining them, with equals signs for the first level and hyphens for the second:

```
How We Dried Plums
==================

The Harvest
-----------
```


Links
=====

The link text goes in square brackets, followed directly by the address in parentheses. After the address you can add a title in quotes, which the browser shows when you hover over the link:

```
We bought the dryer at the [shop](https://example.com/dryer "The shop by the station").
```

When you link to the same address several times, or a long address would clutter the text, use a reference. The text holds only its name, and you write the address anywhere in the article, even at the very end:

```
You'll find the recipe [here][recipe] and Grandma's version [here too][recipe].

[recipe]: https://example.com/recipe
```

To show an address or email exactly as it is, just put it in angle brackets:

```
Write to us at <jane@example.com> or take a look at <https://example.com>.
```

In GFM 🔸, an address without brackets becomes a link too if it starts with `www.`, `http://`, `https://` or `ftp://`, and so does an email address.


Images
======

An image is written just like a link, only with an exclamation mark in front. The text in square brackets describes the image for people who can't see it, and the address may be followed by a title:

```
![Dryer in the attic](dryer.jpg "Our dryer")
```

Markdown has no way to write dimensions, alignment, a CSS class or a caption under the image. If you need them, have a look at [images in Texy syntax |syntax#Images].


Lists
=====

A bulleted list starts with a hyphen, an asterisk or a plus sign, a numbered list with a number followed by a period or a parenthesis. To nest a list, indent it so that it starts under the text of its parent item:

```
- plums
- apples
  - sour
  - sweet

1. pick
2. wash
3. dry
```

A numbered list starts at the number of its first item, so a `3.` at the beginning makes it start at three. Texy works out the following numbers by itself, so you can just as well write `1.` everywhere.

In GFM 🔸, an item becomes a checkbox when it starts with `[ ]` or `[x]`:

```
- [x] pick the plums
- [ ] buy a dryer
```


Blockquotes
===========

A blockquote starts with a `>` at the beginning of the line. Everything else works inside it, including further paragraphs, lists or a nested blockquote:

```
> Dry them, and dry them well.
>
> Grandma
```


Code
====

Wrap inline code in backticks, for example ''`$markdown->toHtml()`''. If the code itself contains a backtick, use two on each side.

Longer code goes in a block between lines of three backticks. After the opening ones you can add the language, which the website uses to highlight the code:

```
 ```php
 $texy = new Texy\Texy;
 ```
```

Text indented by at least four spaces is a code block too. Nothing inside code gets formatted or changed, and typography leaves it alone.


Tables
======

Tables are part of GFM 🔸. Separate the cells with vertical bars, and under the first row, which forms the header, write a separator made of hyphens:

```
| Fruit   | Drying time | Temperature |
|---------|:-----------:|------------:|
| plums   | 12 h        | 60 °C       |
| apples  | 6 h         | 55 °C       |
```

Colons in the separator set the alignment of the column: left `:---`, center `:---:` and right `---:`. The bars don't have to line up; aligning them only makes the source easier to read. Markdown cannot merge table cells, but [tables in Texy syntax |syntax#Tables] can.


Horizontal Rule
===============

Three or more hyphens, asterisks or underscores on a line of their own create a horizontal rule. Hyphens must be separated from the text above them by a blank line, otherwise they would turn that text into a heading.

```
End of part one.

---

Beginning of part two.
```


HTML in Text
============

Where Markdown falls short, you can write HTML directly, both inside text and as a whole block:

```
Text with a <mark>highlight</mark>.

<div class="tip">
Tip: dry them in a thin layer.
</div>
```

The CommonMark specification lets such HTML through to the output unchanged. Texy also makes sure it is well-formed. When an author forgets to close a tag, or closes tags in the wrong order, Texy fixes it and the rest of the page stays intact:

```
Grandma <b>surely <i>loved it</b>.
```

```html
<p>Grandma <b>surely <i>loved it</i></b><i>.</i></p>
```

A tag that HTML doesn't know, such as `<foo-bar>`, is shown as plain text. On a website where visitors write, probably only a few safe tags like `<b>` or `<i>` get through, and the others are shown as text as well. On top of that, GFM 🔸 always defuses tags that could endanger the page, such as `<script>` or `<iframe>`.


Writing Special Characters
==========================

Want to write an asterisk that shouldn't emphasize anything, or a number with a period at the start of a line that shouldn't start a list? Put a backslash in front of the character:

```
\*this is not italic\*

1986\. What a year for plums!
```

A backslash takes away the special meaning of any character this way, for example `\#`, `\[` or `\|`.


What Texy Adds to Markdown
==========================

Everything above is standard Markdown, and any converter that follows the specification would read it the same way. Texy also typesets the text, without you having to write anything differently. Here is what it looks like with the default English locale, `$markdown->config->locale = 'en'`:

```
Our dryer holds 40 kg of fruit and we are open 9-17... "Drop by," says Grandma.
```

```html
<p>Our dryer holds 40&nbsp;kg of fruit and we are open 9–17… “Drop by,” says
Grandma.</p>
```

The hyphen between the numbers became an en dash, the three dots an ellipsis, the straight quotes turned into English curly quotes, and a non-breaking space between the number and its unit keeps them from being split at the end of a line. For clarity we show it here as `&nbsp;`; the real output contains the character itself. With another locale, Texy uses the quotes of that language, and in Czech, Slovak and Polish it also binds one-letter prepositions and conjunctions to the next word. When the website turns it on, it hyphenates long Czech and Slovak words, too. All the adjustments are described under [typography |syntax#Typography] in Texy syntax, and they apply to Markdown just the same.

Typography never touches inline code or code blocks.


When Markdown Is Not Enough
===========================

Markdown is simple by design, and some things just can't be written in it: an image with dimensions, alignment and a caption under it, a CSS class on a paragraph, or merged table cells. For these, Texy has a syntax of its own, which is just as simple at its core and is built with such things in mind:

```texy
[* dryer.jpg 300x200 .(Dryer in the attic)[photo] >] *** Photo: Jane Smith
```

If Markdown is starting to hold you back, have a look at [Texy Syntax |syntax].

